# Self portrait

<small>Genuary 2026, day 13 — prompt by Jos Vromans</small>

<VideoLoop src="/13/hero.mp4" poster="/13/hero-poster.jpg" label="Neon wireframe self portrait: 68 facial landmarks with particles drifting off them" />

A self portrait without showing the photo. OpenCV finds the face, then a
Facemark LBF model fits 68 facial landmarks — jaw, eyebrows, nose, eyes,
mouth. Those landmarks are all that reaches the screen: a wireframe in
eighties neon, constantly shedding particles that drift off and fade, so the
portrait is always dissolving and redrawing itself.

This day turned into a whole family of experiments (six variants live in the
repo): Haar-cascade rectangles, the 68-point debug view, a live webcam version
— and the Bauhaus Clown, where every facial feature becomes a random Bauhaus
shape that reshuffles itself:

<VideoLoop src="/13/bauhaus.mp4" poster="/13/bauhaus-poster.jpg" label="Bauhaus Clown: facial features rendered as random Bauhaus shapes, reshuffling every few seconds" />

```kotlin
// haar cascade finds the face, facemark fits the 68 landmarks
val faces = RectVector()
faceCascade.detectMultiScale(grayMat, faces, 1.05, 2,
    opencv_objdetect.CASCADE_SCALE_IMAGE, Size(50, 50), Size(1200, 1200))

val landmarks = Point2fVectorVector()
facemark.fit(grayMat, faces, landmarks)

// landmarks 0-16: jaw · 17-26: eyebrows · 27-35: nose
// 36-47: eyes · 48-67: mouth -> particles, shapes, anything but the photo
```

